\ ===================== MACHINE/MODEL DEPENDENT SECTION ======================
\ Trying to keep the system as portable across both machines and Forth models
\ as possible. model.ft implements a few key routines (eg. DOCOL) and assembler
\ macros (eg. pushrsp,) to hide the details of the model as much as possible.

\ Design:
\ - Native code compilation
\ - Return stack: RSP in Z; top 128 cells of memory, $ff80 to $ffff.
\ - Data stack: PSP is SP; TOS on stack (PUSH, PEEK etc. are super fast)
\ - Return addresses saved in I (like IP in threaded version).
\ - A B C X Y are free; J is reserved (it gets clobbered by STI)
\   - (No STI, STD aside from that use.)

\ Standard dictionary headers: link, length+immediate+hidden word, len words of
\ name, code field.

\ This is a little surprising, but it makes sense when you think deeper:
\ NATIVE HERE pushes the Host's dictionary pointer.
\ HOST   HERE pushes the Target's dictionary pointer
\ TARGET HERE is the mirror word that compiles the Target xt for HERE (!)
HOST DEFINITIONS

: HERE ( -- ) 99 throw ;
: DP   ( -- ) 98 throw ;

\ First line of code, the entry point. It'll be a jump to COLD eventually.
$8000 # pc set, \ Forcing a two-word jump. The target will be adjusted later.

\ XXX: START HERE: The basic buffering is figured out, I think, but it needs
\ hooks at the start of a nonprimitive `:` and `:noname`, at the end `;`, and
\ as part of the `DOES>` machinery.

\ TODO: An alternative compilation scheme can make CREATEd words more efficient.
\ They can be compiled as pushing a literal and then jumping straight to the
\ DOES> code.

\ DOES> design for native code ===============================================
\ This is easier since it's HOST only with a non-interactive target.
\ When the host executes the CONSTANT mirror, it creates a new name and records
\ the value. When that CONSTANT word is compiled (on the host), it pushes code
\ that just inlines the literal.

\ VARIABLEs are CONSTANTs that push their address instead.
\ Probably the simplest way to approach this is to make the metacompiled code
\ more conditional - just skip the target side of immediates for an AOT-only
\ target.
\ Simple CREATEs are likewise just a constant. VALUEs are a constant address
\ plus a store op.

\ But for a (possibly user code) HOST DOES> - how to implement that in general?
\ Sequence 1 (defining a defining word FOO) happens on the host:
\   - Skipped: Target code for defining a FOO word
\   - Required: Host code for defining a FOO word
\   - Required: Target code for executing a FOO word
\   - Optional: Host code for executing a FOO word
\ Sequence 2 (executing FOO to define a word) happens on the host only.
\   - Executes the host code for the LHS of the DOES>, just regular host Forth.
\ Sequence 3 (executing a FOO word) happens in three ways:
\   - Host, interpreting: executes the host RHS code if it exists.
\   - Host, compiling: compiles code to execute the target RHS!
\   - Target, executing compiled code: runs like anything else.

\ The RHS of the DOES> is compiled like any target nonprimitive word. Then any
\ calls of FOO-defined words are compiled as a two-part sequence:
\ - Push the constant address of *this specific* FOO word.
\ - Call the (nameless) nonprimitive implementing the RHS of DOES>

\ This HOST word is invoked by Host-side DOES> to compile the necessary pieces.
\ VARIABLE '(DOES>)
\ VARIABLE 'EXIT   \ These get populated later.

\ Internal helper that compiles a return into a word. This is called by the
\ inliner for `EXIT` on native code platforms.
\ Not technically part of the model interface with the metacompiler.
\ Compiles an exit from a nonprimitive: Pop RSP into a register, and return.
: return, ( -- )
  +return ;

\ Ends a nonprimitive definition: compile a return and then end the definition.
: EXIT, ( -- ) return, done-comp ;

\ Called during the `HOST ACTS:` for a `DOES>`, during Phase 1, i.e. compilation
\ of a defining word like `CONSTANT`. In general, it must do four things:
\ 1. Close the current definition, like `;` would.
\ 2. Open a new definition and capture its xt.
\ 3. Compile any preamble that the target `DOES>` needs.
\ 4. Return the target-xt for the dodoes-code.

\ The code calling eg. a VALUE does a JSR some-val.
\ That pushes the return address, ie. the next bit of real code, to SP.
\ The target code for each value is written by !dodoes. It does a JSR dodoes.
\ The parameter field is in data space, and the parameter field address follows
\ the JSR does-code in a CREATEd word's code.
\ Runtime stack effect: ( ret-addr pfa -- parameter-field )
: DOES, ( -- xt-does )
  \ For a headless target, there's nothing to compile at the end of the current
  \ definition. Just end the current definition, if any.
  compiling @ IF done-comp THEN
  code-space @   \ Capture the xt of the does code
  begin-comp   +dodoes ;


\ Opens a nonprimitive definition, compiling any boilerplate that it needs.
\ For threaded code, either the address of DOCOL/ENTER or a jump to it.
\ For native code, any preamble that's required to handle the return address.
\ Begin compilation, then append a "starter" node as the first entry.
\ For DCPU-16, the return address is on the hardware stack, which is our data
\ stack. So we pop it and push it to RSP (in the code compiled by +starter).
: ,docol ( -- ) begin-comp   +starter ;

\ Effectively the default does-code for CREATEd words without a DOES>.
\ Called by a JSR dovar, so the saved PC on the stack is the pfa, and under
\ it is the real ret-addr back to the word that called the CREATEd word.
label DOVAR ( ret-addr pfa -- parameter-field )
  pop ra   set,  \ Pop PFA into A
  pop rb   set,  \ Pop return address into B
  [a] push set,  \ Push parameter field
  rb  pc   set,  \ And return to the original caller


\ Compiles the code for a CREATEd word without a DOES> section. It should be
\ two words long, and is followed by the PFA. JSR DOVAR, always using long form.
\ TODO: It would be a lot more efficient to inline.
: ,dovar ( -- ) $7c20 tcode, dovar tcode, ;

\ No inline strings allowed - store them explicitly in code space.
: ,dostring ( c-addr u -- ) 97 throw ;

\ Overwrites the code field (address) of the word at word-xt to point to the
\ ;CODE or DOES> at does-xt.
\ On DTC DCPU-16, this compiles JSR does-xt as two words: $7c20 does-xt
: !dodoes ( does-xt word-xt -- )
  ." !dodoes "   base @ >R hex .s R> base ! cr
  $7c20 over tcode!   tcell+ tcode! ;

\ There's a generic `: t>body  t>pfa tpfa@ ;` that expects t>pfa to advance to
\ either the body itself (data direct) or a pointer to the body (indirect).
\ For the native DCPU, data is direct and a variable is compiled as:
\ [ JSR long-lit | DOVAR | <data> ]
\ so it's 2 cells later.
: t>pfa ( target-xt -- target-body ) 2 tcells + ;

\ TODO: This isn't necessarily required...
: TDOES, ;

\ Stacks and tasks ===========================================================
128 tcells CONSTANT default/rs
256 tcells CONSTANT default/ds

\ Tasks starting at the top of memory and growing down.
0 CONSTANT task-area0

label entry-point 0 asm,

\ Compiles the jump or whatever to entry-point at the end of kmain.
: >entry-point, ( -- )
  \ JSR to the entry-point word, and BRK if it ever returns!
  entry-point [#] jsr,
  $dead # brk, ;
